WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:07.929
A pizza is cut into four
pieces. Are they all quarters?

2
00:00:08.149 --> 00:00:12.608
Only if the pieces are equal. Equal
is the word that does the work.

3
00:00:13.108 --> 00:00:16.869
Here are two shapes. Each
one is cut into four pieces.

4
00:00:17.089 --> 00:00:20.897
Look at this one. Every piece
is exactly the same size.

5
00:00:21.117 --> 00:00:23.764
So these four pieces really are quarters.

6
00:00:23.984 --> 00:00:27.328
Now the other one. This piece
is bigger than the rest.

7
00:00:27.548 --> 00:00:31.542
Four pieces is not enough.
Fractions must be equal parts.

8
00:00:32.042 --> 00:00:36.129
This bar is cut into five equal
pieces, so each one is a fifth.

9
00:00:36.349 --> 00:00:39.971
Colour them in, one at a time.
Watch the top number climb.

10
00:00:40.191 --> 00:00:43.953
Five fifths is not nearly
one whole. It is one whole.

11
00:00:44.173 --> 00:00:50.442
A square cut down the middle. A square
cut corner to corner. A circle cut across.

12
00:00:50.662 --> 00:00:55.678
Every one of them is a half. The
shape does not decide the fraction.

13
00:00:56.178 --> 00:00:59.939
A fraction can be part of a
group, not just part of a shape.

14
00:01:00.159 --> 00:01:03.828
Zara has twelve beads. She
wants a quarter of them.

15
00:01:04.048 --> 00:01:08.227
The bottom number says four, so
share them into four equal piles.

16
00:01:08.447 --> 00:01:12.627
Three beads in every pile. Take
one pile, and that is three.

17
00:01:12.847 --> 00:01:16.562
The bottom number tells you how
many piles, not how many to take.

18
00:01:17.062 --> 00:01:19.709
The line in a fraction is a dividing line.

19
00:01:19.929 --> 00:01:23.134
Three quarters means
three shared between four.

20
00:01:23.354 --> 00:01:27.069
Amina has twenty shillings.
What is three quarters of that?

21
00:01:27.289 --> 00:01:32.212
Divide by the bottom number first.
Twenty shared into four is five.

22
00:01:32.432 --> 00:01:36.193
Then multiply by the top. Three
fives are fifteen shillings.

23
00:01:36.693 --> 00:01:40.966
Two fifths, and one more fifth.
Count the pieces: three fifths.

24
00:01:41.186 --> 00:01:44.854
The bottom number never changes.
It is never three tenths.

25
00:01:45.074 --> 00:01:47.954
Now look at one half,
and five tenths under it.

26
00:01:48.174 --> 00:01:51.332
Both bars are coloured
exactly the same amount.

27
00:01:51.552 --> 00:01:54.152
Bigger numbers do not
make a bigger fraction.

28
00:01:54.652 --> 00:01:58.089
When the bottom numbers match,
the pieces are the same size.

29
00:01:58.309 --> 00:02:02.674
So three fifths is bigger than
two fifths. More pieces wins.

30
00:02:02.894 --> 00:02:06.238
But one quarter is bigger than
one tenth. That sounds wrong.

31
00:02:06.458 --> 00:02:10.127
Sharing between fewer people
gives everybody a bigger piece.

32
00:02:10.627 --> 00:02:14.713
A fraction is equal parts, and
all of them make one whole.

33
00:02:14.933 --> 00:02:18.927
You can take a fraction of a group,
because the line means divide.

34
00:02:19.147 --> 00:02:22.816
Two fractions can be worth the
same, and you can compare them.

35
00:02:23.036 --> 00:02:26.287
Now go and fold some paper
strips, and see the equal parts.
